A||||9||Pyric||Pulling the manifold... is it worth it?||||5||
Z||000000||Pyric||11-19-2001||12:01 PM||||The exhaust manifold in my 85 is all rusty and ugly looking. I was planning on pulling them, cleaning them up, and painting them with heat resistent paint (1500 degree +).<P>However, I don't want to have to pull the engine.  Is it possible to do it without much trouble?  or is it not even worth the effort. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||11-19-2001||12:22 PM||||If your only reason for pulling the manifolds is to clean them, it might not be worth the effort. Odds are that you are going to snap at least one bolt off and that can be a bugger to fix. On the plus side if you have them off you can check for and fix any cracks that may be there and of course port them out for an additional 8 or so HP. Hot coating is better than paint.<BR>My info helped a lot huh?||||reg||1
Z||000002||DR650SE||11-19-2001||02:35 PM||||Phil is right.  Those bolts are old.  And trust me, my engine was out and when you snap a bolt off in the block it sucks.  I had to sit there and drill on the bolt for 3 hours before I could get it out with one of those removers I think called Easy outs.  My hands hated me after that.<P>------------------<BR>AIM:  DR650SE<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||13
Z||000003||Pyric||11-19-2001||02:48 PM||||Good point.   I'll wait until I HAVE to pull the engine for something.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Master_Sushi||11-19-2001||05:08 PM||||Are the bolts just as bad on the 2.5's? I was thinking of taking porting my manifold.<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000005||Mickey_Moose||11-19-2001||05:43 PM||||3 hours to dril out a broken stud?!??<P>Took me only 1 hour to drill out a stud that was broken on the front side with the engine still in the car...<P>...taking them out just paint is a waste of time, now if you had a crack in them, then that is different (might as well port them while they are out) - painting is also a waste, best to get them ceramic coated to help keep them from cracking again.<P>just my 2 cents<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000006||Pyric||11-19-2001||06:38 PM||||In that case, does anyone know where I can get a good set of heat-coated replacements (when the time comes). <BR>I've heard the Sprint manifolds steal some of your torque.||||reg||4
Z||000007||StuGood||11-19-2001||08:03 PM||||Pyric, can I now assume you have a V6 (since you talked about replacement "manifolds" - plural)?  When I decided to work over the manifolds my GT (V6), it hadn't the recall package installed, two bolts (on the same exhaust port) were broken, and there was a crack near that same port.  It was apparent that there were exhaust leaks (from the ticking sound when accelerating).  I felt that if I let it go too much longer, I risked warping the manifold beyond repair, let alone the risk of fire.  That made it an easy decision   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Somehow, just "the manifolds look rusty," wouldn't be reason enough for me, IMHO.  It's a lot of work.  That said, I would recommend that you save some $, and weld+port the existing manifolds.  I did it that way, and I'm very pleased with it.  It has Saturn studs all around, I painted the manifolds, painted the valve covers and plenum, along with some of the bracketry.  I am very pleased with the improvement in looks, performance and reliability.  Took her on a 1200-mile trip with complete confidence.  Obviously, I'm kind of proud of the outcome!  Just one person's experience...<P>EDIT (Can't believe I forgot to mention this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ): Yes, it is definitely possible to R&R the exhaust manifolds w/o removing the engine.  I <B>did</B> lower the aft end of the cradle to gain access to drill out one of the broken bolts, though.  BTW, the broken bolts were at cylinder #6. <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000008||DR650SE||11-19-2001||09:17 PM||||Yea it took 3 hours of drilling and some rest, My bit was a little dull and would work less once it got hot.  It sucked||||reg||8
Z||000009||Pyric||11-19-2001||09:18 PM||||Thanks for the info, I was looking at cleaning up the manifolds because I have rebuilt the top side of the engine, and I was considering turning it into a show car.  (no one around here has really got one in good shape).  <BR>I was considering doing this as a later project but now I think I'll just look into some nice upgraded headers that can decrease the engine compartment temperature while looking good (and keeping the classic Pontiac sound). <P>------------------<BR>"Never offend people with style when you can offend them with substance." <BR>--Sam Brown||||reg||10
